{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfj6fTFVY9TTZYMGr9yRq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AWJbCQm7Dj6t2Sajibow3z","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"D=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_2_repair_1\nM=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/models\ncat > $D/hypothesis.md << 'EOF'\nPeople judge a sequence random to the extent their own model of a fair coin (with a person-specific believed switch rate, and a second-order belief about switching twice in a row) explains it better than the regular generators (a switch-biased coin, a biased coin, and a short motif repeated with slips, with each person's own suspicion of motifs). The single distortion is a neglect of Occam's razor: instead of averaging over every rate a trick coin might have, people partly credit the trick coins with whatever switch rate or heads rate best fits the sequence at hand (a fitted share between the normative average and the best fit), so any sequence whose rates are even mildly lopsided is readily \"explained\" by a tailor-made trick coin, and moderately imbalanced or moderately streaky sequences look less random than the normative account implies, relative to perfectly typical ones.\nEOF\necho occam_neglect_trick_coin_fit > $D/model_name.txt\ncp $M/second_order_chance_motif_suspicion.py $D/candidate.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7316,"cache_read_input_tokens":67731,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7316,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2c275e